            <ArticleTitle>Aortic pulsatility assessed by an oscillometric method is associated with coronary atherosclerosis in elderly people.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>373-380</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The aim of this study was to investigate the association of aortic pulsatility assessed by a non-invasive brachial cuff-based method with coronary atherosclerosis. In total, 139 patients undergoing coronary angiography were included in this cross-sectional study. Aortic blood pressure (BP) indices were recorded invasively by a fluid-filled catheter and non-invasively by a brachial cuff-based oscillometric device. Fractional pulse pressure (FPP) was defined as pulse pressure (PP)/mean BP and pulsatility index (PI) as PP/diastolic BP. Aortic FPP and PI in coronary artery disease (CAD) patients were significantly higher than in non-CAD patients in both invasive and non-invasive methods. Multivariate logistic regression analysis demonstrated that non-invasively measured aortic FPP and PI were associated with CAD risk in patients aged ≥70 years [aortic FPP per 0.1 odds ratio (OR) = 1.66, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.05-2.64; aortic PI per 0.1 OR =1.39, 95% CI 1.02-1.88; all p &lt; 0.05], but were not associated with CAD risk in patients aged &lt;70 years. In linear regression analysis, non-invasively measured aortic FPP and PI correlated with SYNTAX and Gensini scores only in patients aged ≥70 years. Aortic FPP and PI measured non-invasively by a brachial cuff-based oscillometric device were associated with coronary atherosclerosis in elderly patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
